Now I understand `flb_utf8_len`. For a byte >= 0x80, it can return up to 6 (for 0xF8-0xFF, trailingBytes = 5, +1 = 6). 

Now let me reconsider the `flb_utils_write_str` overflow. The key issue is in the `c >= 0x80 && c <= 0xFFFF` branch:

```c
else if (c >= 0x80 && c <= 0xFFFF) {
    hex_bytes = flb_utf8_len(str + i);
    if (available - written < 6) {
        return FLB_FALSE;
    }
    if (i + hex_bytes > str_len) {
        break; /* skip truncated UTF-8 */
    }
    ...
    len = snprintf(tmp, sizeof(tmp) - 1, "\\u%.4x", codepoint);
    encoded_to_buf(p, tmp, len);
    p += len;
    ...
    i += (hex_bytes - 1);
}
```

The check `available - written < 6` ensures at least 6 bytes available. `len` is 6. So it writes 6 bytes. Safe.

